    "lawCode": "Amd §§3623-a, 3602 & 1709, Ed L; amd §§1115 & 1210, Tax L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5268\n\nSPONSOR: KENNEDY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the education law and the tax law, in relation to allow-\ning for reimbursement of school districts for the purchase or lease of\nelectric school buses\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nAssists districts in covering the cost of purchasing electric school\nbuses\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Details the findings of the Legislature in regards to lessen-\ning the effects of climate change through electrifying school transpor-\ntation.\n\nSection 2: Amends subdivision 1 of section 3623-a of the education law.\n\nSection 3: Amends subparagraph 1 of paragraph b of subdivision 7 of\nsection 3602 of the education law.\n\nSection 4: Amends subdivision 7 of section 3602 of the education law.\n\nSection 5: Amends subdivision (a) of section 1115 of the tax law.\n\nSection 6: Amends subparagraph 1 of subdivision 25 of section 1709 of\nthe education law.\n\nSection 7: Amends subparagraph (11) of paragraph 1 of subdivision (a) of\nsection 1310 of the tax law.\n\nSection 8: Sets the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAs of 2016, the great majority of the State's greenhouse gas emissions\nderive from fuel combustion, with the largest contributor, at 36%,\ncoming from vehicle fuel combustion in the transportation sector (New\nYork State Greenhouse Gas Inventory 1990-2016). Composing more than a\nthird of greenhouse pollution in the state, vehicle emissions constitute\nan issue to environmental conservation, sustainability, and public\nhealth. Our State's extensive bus network, although crucial to connect-\ning our communities, currently imposes an alarmingly high amount of\npollution in its diesel format.\n\nThe electrification of buses - both transit and school buses - provides\na reliable and cost-effective option for cities and school districts,\nall while reducing the environmental and health threats posed by diesel\nfuels. The financial burden of transitioning between diesel and electric\npower is the biggest obstacle preventing a full transition to electric,\nand it is the responsibility of the legislature to assist our overloaded\nschool districts with all costs incurred or related to the installation\nof electric vehicle charging infrastructure, cost of electricity for\ncharging vehicles, or the purchasing or leasing of the vehicles them-\nselves.\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nTBD\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
